<Base
  title="Enshrouded Sparkworks Guide: Levers, Pressure Plates and Logic Gates"
  description="Sparkworks is the Enshrouded 1.0 logic system that wires levers and pressure plates to doors, traps and enemy spawns using Switchboxes, Clocks and logic gates."
>
  <p><span class="tag">Enshrouded 1.0</span> <span class="tag">Building</span> <span class="tag">Last verified 2026-10-14</span></p>
  <h1>Enshrouded Sparkworks Guide: Levers, Pressure Plates and Logic Gates</h1>

  <p class="muted">
    <strong>Sparkworks</strong> is the new logic system arriving with Enshrouded 1.0 on October 15, 2026.
    It connects <strong>inputs</strong> (levers, pressure plates, triggers) to <strong>outputs</strong>
    (doors, trapdoors, traps, lightning strikes, released water, enemy spawns) so a base can become a
    puzzle, a trap course or a complete custom dungeon. Because it hooks into Adventure Sharing, other
    players can visit what you build.
  </p>

  <div class="toc">
    <strong>On this page</strong>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#what">What Sparkworks is</a></li>
      <li><a href="#inputs">Inputs (what can trigger it)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#outputs">Outputs (what can happen)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#parts">The parts: Switchboxes, Sparkworks Tool, Clocks</a></li>
      <li><a href="#example">Build your first mechanism</a></li>
      <li><a href="#sharing">Adventure Sharing and Share Codes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what">What Sparkworks is</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      Enshrouded has always been a building game, but bases were static. Sparkworks adds a logic layer
      on top of building: you place a trigger, you place something that should react, and you wire the
      two together. The result is a mechanism — and once mechanisms exist, players can chain them into
      dungeons, puzzles and mini-games, then publish the whole world for others to play.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="inputs">Inputs (what can trigger it)</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Input</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Lever</td><td>Manual switch — pull it to fire the connected action.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pressure plate</td><td>Fires when a player steps on it, so it can be hidden in a corridor or floor.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Trigger volume</td><td>Fires when another player enters an area, which is what makes "walk into the room and something happens" setups possible.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="muted">
    Inputs can be combined rather than used one at a time — that is where <strong>Clocks</strong> and
    <strong>logic gates</strong> come in, letting you require conditions to be true together or in a
    specific order.
  </p>

  <h2 id="outputs">Outputs (what can happen)</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Output</th><th>Use</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Doors</td><td>Open or close on trigger — the classic "lever opens the gate" puzzle.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Trapdoors</td><td>Drop a floor section under someone's feet.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Traps</td><td>Armed by a plate, fired at whoever triggers it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lightning strikes</td><td>Environmental damage in an outdoor or open-roof space.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Released water</td><td>Flood a section — useful for puzzles that gate progress.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Enemy spawns</td><td>Trigger creatures as part of an encounter, i.e. an actual fight you designed.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="parts">The parts: Switchboxes, Sparkworks Tool, Clocks</h2>
  <div class="grid grid-2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Switchbox</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        The connection point. You place switchboxes to link a trigger to the object it should control —
        effectively the wiring hub of a mechanism.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Sparkworks Tool</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        The tool used to set up those connections. This is what turns a pile of props into a working
        contraption rather than a decorative build.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Clocks</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Timed logic. Useful for sequences — something opens, waits, then something else fires.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Logic gates</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Conditional logic, so an action only happens when the conditions you set are met. This is the
        piece that makes real puzzles and multi-step dungeons possible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="example">Build your first mechanism</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Place a trigger.</strong> Start with a lever — it is the easiest to test because you control when it fires.</li>
    <li><strong>Place the object to control.</strong> A door is the clearest first target.</li>
    <li><strong>Wire them with a Switchbox.</strong> Use the Sparkworks Tool to connect the lever to the door.</li>
    <li><strong>Test it.</strong> Pull the lever and confirm the door responds before you add more parts.</li>
    <li><strong>Swap the lever for a pressure plate.</strong> Now the mechanism fires from player movement instead of a manual action — that single change is what turns a switch into a trap.</li>
    <li><strong>Add a Clock or logic gate.</strong> Only once the basics work, add timing or conditions.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The staging matters: test one connection at a time. Most confusion around logic systems comes from
    wiring five things at once and not knowing which link is wrong.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sharing">Adventure Sharing and Share Codes</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      Sparkworks is designed to feed Enshrouded's existing <strong>Adventure Sharing</strong> feature,
      where players upload worlds for others to visit. With 1.0, mechanisms make it possible to publish:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Custom dungeons with real encounters</li>
      <li>Puzzle courses and trap runs</li>
      <li>Mini-games built inside the world</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On the discovery side you can browse <strong>Popular Adventures</strong>, filter by tags, enter a
      <strong>Share Code</strong> to jump straight to a specific world, and subscribe to favourites.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0" class="muted">
      Note: cross-platform multiplayer is not available at 1.0 launch according to Keen Games. Adventure
      Sharing behaviour on multiplayer servers has not been detailed in the pre-launch notes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>What is Sparkworks in Enshrouded?</strong><br />A 1.0 logic system that links triggers such as levers and pressure plates to outcomes such as doors, traps and enemy spawns, using Switchboxes, the Sparkworks Tool, Clocks and logic gates.</p>
    <p><strong>Is Sparkworks paid DLC?</strong><br />No. It ships with the free 1.0 full release on October 15, 2026. Early Access owners on PC do not need to buy the game again.</p>
    <p><strong>Can I make a dungeon with it?</strong><br />Yes — that is the point. Triggers, timer logic and enemy spawns are enough to build encounters; Adventure Sharing is how others play them.</p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Does Sparkworks work on servers?</strong><br />Keen Games has not stated details for multiplayer servers in the pre-launch material.</p>
  </div>

